---
/**
 * /replicate: the toolkit's yes to "could we have this where we live?"
 * (BR22). The reader who matters is someone organizing in another place:
 * the page gives the yes, the picture of their own copy, and one ask. The
 * developer steps come last. The corner door, the footer, and the cover's
 * exits point here.
 */
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const CONTACT_EMAIL = 'resiliencetoolkit@gocros.org';
const mailto = `mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Bringing the toolkit to [your town]')}`;
const issueUrl =
  'https://github.com/000noyes/resiliencetoolkit.org/issues/new?template=bring-it-to-your-town.md&title=' +
  encodeURIComponent('Bring it to your town: ');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Bring it to your town | Resilience Hub Toolkit"
  description="Most of the Resilience Hub Toolkit works anywhere. What a copy keeps, what would change for your place, and how to ask for one."
>
  <article class="container mx-auto px-4 py-8 max-w-3xl">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <h1 class="text-hero font-semibold mb-4 text-foreground">Yes, you can bring it to your town.</h1>
      <p class="text-body-large text-text-secondary">
        This toolkit was written in Vermont after the floods of 2023 and 2024. Most of it works
        anywhere people organize to look after each other.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="space-y-10">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-title font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">What would change</h2>
        <p class="text-body text-foreground mb-3">
          A copy for your place keeps everything that makes the toolkit work. It reads offline once
          opened. Notes and checklists stay on the reader's device. It prints as the workbook. Four
          things are Vermont's, and a new community swaps them:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-body text-foreground">
          <li>The Vermont Town Directory, a card in the Downloads room and a row under Knowing Your Community in every contents list.</li>
          <li>The state map links in 0.1 and on the Map page.</li>
          <li>The flood story on the cover and on About.</li>
          <li>Local contacts inside chapters 1.8, 1.9, 1.11, 2.1, 2.2, and 2.3.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-title font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">Ask us</h2>
        <p class="text-body text-foreground mb-3">
          <a href={mailto}>Write to {CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. Say where you are and what your group does.
        </p>
        <p class="text-body text-foreground">
          If you can set up a website, the steps are below, and you can
          <a href={issueUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">open a request on GitHub</a> instead.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-title font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">For developers</h2>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-body text-foreground">
          <li>
            Fork <a href="https://github.com/000noyes/resiliencetoolkit.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/000noyes/resiliencetoolkit.org</a>, then:
            <pre class="bg-muted rounded-lg p-4 mt-2 text-body-small overflow-x-auto"><code>git clone https://github.com/YOUR-ORG/resiliencetoolkit.org.git
cd resiliencetoolkit.org
pnpm install
pnpm dev</code></pre>
          </li>
          <li>
            Where things live: chapters under <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">src/pages/modules/</code>,
            titles and order in <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">src/data/contents.ts</code>,
            the workbook in <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">public/toolkit/</code>,
            page ranges in <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">docs/source-specs/</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            The build checks chapter text against the workbook
            (<code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">pnpm verify</code>). It reads a committed
            extraction cache, so the host needs no PDF tools. A localized copy replaces the workbook and its
            page ranges and refreshes the cache locally with pdftotext, or sets its build command to
            <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">pnpm astro build</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Deploy on Cloudflare Pages, the tested path: build command
            <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">pnpm build</code>, output
            <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">dist</code>. The review-round API under
            <code class="bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-chrome">functions/</code> is optional and needs a D1
            binding only if the copy runs review rounds. Other static hosts should work but are untested.
          </li>
          <li>
            The code is under the MIT License. The content license is the coalition's to set; ask when you write.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <p class="text-body-small text-text-secondary border-t border-border pt-4">
        Built from the <a href="https://resiliencetoolkit.org">Resilience Hub Toolkit</a> by Community Resilience Organizations (CROs), Vermont.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
